INT. LONG-TERM CARE HOME - PRIVATE ROOM - LATE AFTERNOON

A single bed, neatly made. A rocking chair by the window. A tray of untouched dinner on the side table. Photographs crowd the dresser: a wedding, a fishing trip, a toddler on a pony. The window frames a brick wall and a sliver of sky.

AUGUSTUS "GUS" WELLINGTON-ABARA, 80s, in a cardigan and slippers, sits in the rocking chair with a landline phone in his lap. The cord stretches to the wall. He stares at the receiver as if it might speak first.

He lifts it. Dials slowly, one number at a time, with a shaky forefinger. It rings.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone, young, brisk)
Hello?

                    GUS
Hello. Is this Daniel?

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Who's calling?

                    GUS
It's your father.

A long pause on the line. Gus watches the brick wall. A pigeon lands on the sill, ruffles its feathers, takes off again.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Dad. It's been a while.

                    GUS
It has.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Is something wrong?

                    GUS
No. I just wanted to hear your voice.

Silence. Gus sets the dinner tray aside, lifts the cover. Cold peas. He puts the cover back.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
I can't talk long. I'm at work.

                    GUS
Of course. I understand.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Is the staff treating you well?

                    GUS
They are very kind.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Good.

Gus rocks. The chair creaks. He runs his thumb along the phone cord, back and forth.

                    GUS
I found your old baseball glove.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
You kept that?

                    GUS
In the closet. It still smells like leather.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
God. I haven't thought about that in years.

                    GUS
You pitched a shutout in it. Nineteen eighty-four.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Eighty-five.

                    GUS
Eighty-five.

He smiles, and his eyes go to the dresser, to a faded photograph of a small boy in an oversized cap, scowling at the camera. He picks up the frame, wipes the glass with his sleeve.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Dad, I really have to go.

                    GUS
Yes. Of course. Thank you for answering.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'll call you Sunday.

                    GUS
I'd like that.

The line clicks. He holds the receiver against his ear a moment longer, listening to the empty hum.

He puts the phone down gently on its cradle. Sets the photograph back on the dresser, straightens it. He crosses to the window and stands there, hands clasped behind his back, looking at the brick.

After a while, he sits again in the rocking chair, takes the cold fork from the tray, and begins, slowly, to eat the peas.
